  Left out of AnuLM-Omni-400M on purpose:

  * Flickr30k: non-commercial research and education only.
  * A-OKVQA and its COCO photos: the photos carry their Flickr owners'
    licences, some of them non-commercial.
  * IndicTTS-Hindi: the SPRINGLab card states no licence and defers to the
    IIT Madras Indic TTS licence, whose commercial terms are unclear
    (INCLUDE_INDICTTS = False in omni_data.py).
  * The synthetic Indian-banking set: its templates were written with
    Claude, and Anthropic's terms forbid using outputs to train models
    without authorization.
  * The vision projector learned on Flickr30k: Omni's image projector starts
    fresh. Its audio projector starts from ckpt_asr_en_v3's, which learned
    from LibriSpeech (CC BY 4.0) only.
  * indic-instruct-data-v0.1: its anudesh subset holds Llama 2 outputs. It
    was only used by chat v4 and v5, which were never released.

  The earlier per-task checkpoints -- chat v3, the Decide adapters, the
  reader, English and Hindi ASR, caption and VQA -- exist in this
  repository's history and logs, and the table above records what each
  learned from, but they are not released.

  The sharpest points:

  * `ckpt_coder` and `ckpt_coder_sft` learned from code_exercises, which is
    CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and was generated with ChatGPT 3.5. That makes the coder
    weights non-commercial and share-alike, and a downstream model trained
    on their outputs may inherit the same terms.

  * `ckpt_translate` was trained on Samanantar and the IIT Bombay corpus,
    both of which carry non-commercial terms. It is the best-performing model
    in this project and it is the one NOT to ship commercially without
    resolving that.

  * `ckpt_coder` learned from codeparrot-clean, which is de-duplicated GitHub
    Python and therefore contains GPL- and AGPL-licensed source. Whether that
    propagates to model weights is unsettled. ckpt_base_v2 and everything
    tuned from it, AnuLM-Omni-400M included, carry the same question.

  * OpenCoder-LLM/opc-sft-stage1, in ckpt_base_v2, says MIT on its card but
    includes filtered_infinity_instruct, derived from BAAI/Infinity-Instruct
    (CC BY-SA 4.0), and realuser_instruct, drawn from ShareGPT and WildChat
    (ChatGPT conversations). This and the codeparrot question above are the
    two open questions AnuLM-Omni-400M inherits from base v2; neither is
    settled.

  * `ckpt_caption_v3` and `ckpt_vqa_v3` learned from Flickr30k, whose terms
    allow non-commercial research and education only, and the VQA model also
    from COCO photos. That is why neither is released and why
    AnuLM-Omni-400M's captions and image questions come from other sources.

  Whether training on copyrighted material creates any claim over the
  resulting weights is genuinely unsettled law and differs by jurisdiction.
  Nothing here is legal advice. For research, teaching, portfolio or internal
  use none of it is a problem; AnuLM-Omni-400M is the one model built for
  commercial use, which is why its data list above is narrower.

